(function(){if(["/bar-accessories/", "/led-light-up-cups/", "/custom-silicone-cups/", "/custom-shot-glasses/"].includes(location.pathname))return;var c={"/custom-plastic-cups/":[{"q":"How do I choose plastic cups with lids and straws?","a":"Use the exact model and component references. Nominal capacity alone does not establish lid or straw fit. Confirm the chosen components and packing in the specification."},{"q":"Do you supply blank cups for sublimation or resale?","a":"Blank tumblers, can coolers and pint cups are supplied for resellers and decorators. Tell us which decoration method you use — heat transfer, UV DTF wrap or direct print — and we confirm a compatible surface before you order."},{"q":"Can you print Tritan water bottles and handled tumblers?","a":"Tritan bottles and handled tumblers are printed by screen or UV printing depending on the surface and artwork. Send the model reference and quantity and we confirm the method and print area."},{"q":"Are colour-changing cups available in bulk?","a":"Colour-changing and mood cups are produced in bulk over plastic bodies. Confirm the base model and capacity first, because the colour-change layer is applied to a specific body."}],"/custom-silicone-cups/":[{"q":"Which reference can I use for silicone shot glasses?","a":"LJB-001 is the silicone shot-cup reference in the current model library. Review its model page, then confirm the final dimensions, logo and quantities for your project."},{"q":"What is the smallest silicone order you can run?","a":"Ten pieces on a mould we already hold, when the change is colour or print only. A new silicone shape needs its own compression mould, which starts at 10,000 pieces because the mould has to be cut and tried before the first saleable part."}],"/led-light-up-cups/":[{"q":"Can LED cups with logo share the same power and lighting?","a":"No common configuration is assumed. State the desired lighting, activation and power preference, then review each selected model. The logo should be checked with the light on and off."},{"q":"How should I specify activation and power?","a":"Describe the venue workflow, desired effect and power preference, then ask the team to confirm the exact model configuration and instructions."},{"q":"Which LED cup styles can be printed?","a":"Beer cups, rocks and shot cups, stemware and tumblers can all take a printed logo where the body has a flat printable panel. On a curved or faceted body the usable area is smaller than it looks - it is confirmed on the proof before production."},{"q":"How is the light switched on?","a":"Switching differs by model: some use a button, some a twist base, some a pull tab. Confirm the activation method for the model you choose before the artwork is approved, because it changes where the logo can sit."},{"q":"What power configuration do the cups use?","a":"Power is model-specific - button cells, sealed battery compartments and rechargeable versions are all in the range. Ask for the configuration of the model you are quoting, and whether the cell is replaceable, before you plan a multi-day event."},{"q":"What is the minimum order for an LED cup?","a":"Eligible stock models start from 10 pieces; a new body or a different lighting position means a new mould, which starts from 10,000 pieces. Your quotation states the figure for the model and the lighting you have chosen."}],"/bar-accessories/":[{"q":"Are silicone sleeves, neoprene sleeves and foam can coolers interchangeable?","a":"No. They are different material or construction references. Match the intended can, bottle or glass, then confirm the material and decoration for the specific model."},{"q":"Do you make slim can koozies?","a":"Slim can sizes are produced alongside standard can coolers. Send the can dimensions or a physical sample so the sleeve is matched to the actual container."},{"q":"Can I order personalised koozies in bulk?","a":"Personalised and bulk koozie orders run on the same line in silicone and neoprene. Send the quantity, artwork and container dimensions."},{"q":"Are neoprene and zip-up bottle koozies available?","a":"Neoprene styles and zip-up bottle sleeves are available. Construction and closure are confirmed against your bottle before production."}],"/wholesale/":[{"q":"How many units count as a wholesale order?","a":"There are three thresholds, and they are not interchangeable: 10 pieces on a mould we already hold when only colour or printing changes; 250 pieces when the item needs colour-change ink; 10,000 pieces when a new mould has to be cut. A wholesale price ladder usually starts to make sense from 500 pieces per model per colour."},{"q":"What has to be written in a bulk quotation?","a":"Quantity per model, quantity per colour, number of artwork versions, packing method, delivery term and the lead time that goes with that quantity. A single unit price without those six items cannot be compared with another supplier's price, because the print set-up and the packing are where most of the difference hides."},{"q":"Can I mix several models in one bulk order?","a":"Yes, and most distributors do. The catch is that the minimum applies to each model and each colour, not to the order total: 300 pieces of one model in three colours is three minimums, not one. We quote the mixed order line by line so you can see where the volume is actually sitting."},{"q":"Why does the unit price drop so much between 500 and 5,000 pieces?","a":"Three costs are fixed and get divided by the quantity: the print set-up per colour, the machine start-up time, and any tooling that has to be amortised. Material is the only cost that scales almost linearly. That is why the first thousand pieces carry most of the fixed cost and the last four thousand mostly carry material."}],"/private-label-drinkware/":[{"q":"Can you print our brand on the product and on the retail box?","a":"Both. Product decoration is printed, and the retail box can carry your logo, barcode and your own product code. Send the artwork, the barcode number and the box dimensions with the order — box artwork is approved on a printed proof before the run, not after."},{"q":"Can the outer carton be neutral, shipped straight to our warehouse?","a":"Yes. Export cartons can go out blank or with a shipping mark you specify, and we can label each carton with your SKU and barcode so your warehouse can receive it without re-packing. Tell us the label layout and we build it into the packing instruction."},{"q":"Who owns the mould if we pay for it?","a":"A customer-funded mould is treated as yours for the life of the project, and the exclusivity terms are written into the quotation rather than left verbal. The mould stays in our tooling room so we can run replacement orders, and the terms for moving it are agreed before you pay."},{"q":"Can a private-label range start at 250 pieces per SKU?","a":"Only on items where the body already exists and the change is colour or print. 250 pieces is the colour-change threshold. Anything that needs a new shape is a tooling project at 10,000 pieces, whatever the brand on the box says."}],"/low-moq-custom-cups/":[{"q":"What is the smallest order you will actually accept?","a":"Ten pieces, on a mould we already hold, with colour or printing changed. Below ten the set-up cost is spread over too few units to be worth quoting, so small quantities are normally handled as paid samples rather than as an order."},{"q":"Why does a 10-piece order cost so much per cup?","a":"Because almost none of the cost is plastic. A screen set-up is charged per colour whether you print 10 cups or 10,000; the machine still needs loading, running and cleaning; and the packing is done by hand. At 10 pieces you are mostly paying for the set-up, which is why the per-unit price falls steeply by 500."},{"q":"Which products can start at ten pieces?","a":"Rigid plastic and silicone items that are already in the model library, in a stock colour or a stock size, with a one or two colour print. LED items can start low only where the light module is a stock part. Anything requiring a new shape, a new colour match or a new ink is a different threshold."},{"q":"Can I get ten pieces with my logo to test a market?","a":"Yes, and it is a sensible way to test. At that quantity the print may be done by a short-run method rather than screen printing, so the finish can differ slightly from the production run. If the test is meant to predict the final look, say so and we quote the production method instead."}],"/custom-tumblers/":[{"q":"Which tumbler body should I choose for hot drinks?","a":"Double-wall for hot, single-wall for cold. A single-wall body passes heat straight to the hand and will condensation on the outside with iced drinks. Check the lid seal as well as the body: a press-fit lid that holds water upside down is the difference between a usable tumbler and a complaint."},{"q":"Can a tumbler be printed all the way round?","a":"Screen printing wraps one or two colours around a limited arc; a full 360° full-colour wrap needs UV printing or heat transfer. On a tapered body the artwork has to be distorted to follow the curve, so send the dieline or let us build one from the model dimensions."},{"q":"Will the tumbler fit a car cup holder?","a":"Only if the base diameter is small enough for your holder, and that is a dimension, not a category. Ask for the base diameter of the specific model before you commit — two tumblers of the same capacity can differ by 15 mm at the base and only one will drop in."},{"q":"Is the print dishwasher safe?","a":"It depends on the ink and the body, not on the word tumbler. Standard screen ink on PP survives hand washing comfortably and may fade in a commercial dishwasher; UV print on Tritan is more resistant. If the tumblers go through a machine daily, say so at quotation stage and we quote the ink that suits it."}],"/custom-ice-buckets/":[{"q":"Which ice bucket size suits a table service?","a":"A 5 litre bucket covers a two to four bottle table service with ice around the bottles; a 10 litre bucket is what most bars use for a full shift. Check the internal diameter against your bottle diameter - a wide bucket with a narrow opening is useless for champagne."},{"q":"Is a printed ice bucket still safe for drinks?","a":"Yes, because the decoration goes on the outside only. The inside surface stays the base material with no ink and no coating. We do not print the interior of any item that holds ice, drink or food, and we will say so in writing if a design asks for it."},{"q":"Acrylic or PP for an ice bucket?","a":"Acrylic gives the clear, glass-like look that suits a branded table and shows the ice; PP is opaque, lighter and cheaper to run for volume promotions. Both are on this page. Choose by how the bucket will be seen and handled, then confirm capacity and packing per model."},{"q":"Can the bucket come with a lid and a liner?","a":"Some models have a lid or an inner liner, and some do not - it is a per-model detail, not a page-wide promise. Tell us the model you are considering and we confirm the components, the lid fit and how the liner is packed before you approve a sample."},{"q":"Can one order mix LED and non-LED models?","a":"Yes. LED and non-LED buckets can be ordered together on one purchase order, and each model keeps its own minimum quantity. Packing is separated per model so cartons can go to different venues or rooms."},{"q":"What is the minimum order for a printed ice bucket?","a":"Eligible stock models start from 10 pieces with a print or colour change; a new mould starts from 10,000 pieces. The figure depends on the model you choose - it is confirmed in the quotation rather than assumed from this page."},{"q":"How is the logo applied to a plastic ice bucket?","a":"Screen printing and UV printing are the usual methods, and heat transfer is used where the artwork needs more colours. The printable area is the flat panel of the bucket; on a shaped or ribbed body we confirm the usable area on the proof before production."},{"q":"Can the buckets be packed for a specific event date?","a":"Tell us the date the goods must arrive and where they are going. Production and shipping are planned backwards from that date, and cartons can be marked per venue. Rush timing is quoted case by case, not promised on the page."}],"/custom-wine-glasses/":[{"q":"Are these real glass?","a":"No. Every wine glass on this site is plastic — PP, Tritan or acrylic styled as a stemmed or stemless glass. We do not make or supply glass, ceramic or metal drinkware, so if the project needs real glass we are the wrong supplier and will say so at the first message."},{"q":"Stemmed or stemless for an event?","a":"Stemless survives a crowded bar and packs flat; stemmed reads as a proper wine service and snaps at the joint if it is dropped. For a 500-seat reception, stemless in Tritan is the lower-risk choice. For a tasting with seated guests, stemmed is worth the extra care."},{"q":"Can the bowl be printed without the ink washing off?","a":"On Tritan and PP the print is applied to the outside of the bowl and cured; on thin acrylic the print area is limited. The bowl curve distorts artwork, so a logo sits best on a flat or gently curved panel. Tell us the cleaning routine and we match the ink to it."},{"q":"How many wine glasses fit in a carton?","a":"It depends on the nesting and the wall thickness, and it changes the freight more than the unit price does. Ask for the carton count and the carton weight for the exact model — a glass that nests saves roughly a third of the volume of one that does not."}],"/custom-champagne-flutes/":[{"q":"What makes a plastic flute look like a real one?","a":"Three things: a thin rim, a tulip-shaped bowl rather than a straight tube, and a stem that is moulded in one piece with the bowl. A two-piece glued stem is visible at the joint and fails first. Ask which construction the model uses before you compare prices."},{"q":"Can flutes be packed for a toast service?","a":"Yes. Flutes are usually packed in a moulded tray so they arrive upright and unmarked, and can be supplied in a printed sleeve for a table setting. Packing adds cost per unit but cuts breakage claims, which is usually the better trade for a wedding or a launch."},{"q":"Do flutes work with colour-change ink?","a":"Yes, and it is a popular combination over a plastic flute body — the colour develops as the chilled drink goes in. The ink layer sits on the outside of the bowl; the rim and the inside stay clear."},{"q":"What is the minimum for a custom flute?","a":"Ten pieces on an existing flute mould with a colour or print change, 250 pieces for colour-change ink, 10,000 pieces for a new flute shape. Most champagne projects land in the 500 to 2,000 range, which sits inside the existing-mould tier."}],"/custom-bottle-openers/":[{"q":"Plastic or metal bottle openers?","a":"We tool and mould the plastic version in ABS and similar engineering plastics, and that is the line we can control from mould to print. Metal openers are outside our material scope, so we will not quote a stainless or zinc-alloy body even if a catalogue shows one."},{"q":"Will a plastic opener survive real use?","a":"The load sits in the lip that catches the crown, so that is where the design matters: a thick lip in ABS handles bar use, a thin decorative lip does not. Ask for the wall thickness at the lip. An opener that is bought as a giveaway and an opener that lives behind a bar are two different specifications."},{"q":"Can the opener carry a logo on both sides?","a":"Yes, and on the handle as well if the model has one. Two-colour printing on a small flat face is the practical limit for screen work; finer detail needs pad printing or UV. Send the artwork at final size so we can tell you whether the fine lines will hold."},{"q":"Can the opener be part of a gift set?","a":"Yes. Openers are commonly packed with a bottle stopper, a pourer or a coaster in a printed box or a pouch. Each component has its own quantity and its own decoration, so the set is quoted as a bill of materials rather than as one price."}],"/acrylic-drinkware/":[{"q":"What does acrylic give that PP does not?","a":"Clarity. Acrylic reads as glass from a metre away, which is why hotels and pool bars use it. PP is cheaper, tougher and slightly milky. The trade is real: acrylic scratches, and a scratched acrylic tumbler looks worse than a plain PP one, so it is a front-of-house material, not a beach material."},{"q":"Can acrylic be washed in a dishwasher?","a":"Hand wash is the safe answer, and for commercial dishwashers the honest answer is no. Heat and alkaline detergent cloud the surface and can craze it over repeated cycles. If the item will be machine washed daily, we will steer you to PP or Tritan instead."},{"q":"Does the print hold on acrylic?","a":"It holds when it is applied and cured correctly, and it is more sensitive to solvents than print on PP. Avoid printing near the rim where lips and repeated washing touch. Flat panels and the lower third of the body are the reliable areas."},{"q":"Why do acrylic items cost more than the same shape in PP?","a":"The resin costs more, the moulding window is narrower, and scrap rates are higher because acrylic is less forgiving of a cold shot. A reject acrylic part cannot be reground into the same clear product, so the scrap has to be paid for somewhere."}],"/unbreakable-drinkware/":[{"q":"What does unbreakable actually mean here?","a":"It means the item does not shatter into glass-like shards. It does not mean it cannot be damaged. A PP tumbler dropped on concrete can crack or scuff; a Tritan one usually survives. Ask for the drop height the model was tested at rather than accepting the word unbreakable as a specification."},{"q":"One-piece or two-piece construction?","a":"One-piece moulding has no joint to fail and no place for liquid to sit, so it is the better choice for pools, stadiums and anywhere the cup goes into a sink. Two-piece items exist for shape reasons, mainly stemmed glasses, and the joint is the first thing to check on a sample."},{"q":"Tritan, PP or PS for a venue?","a":"Tritan for clarity plus toughness, PP for the lowest cost and the highest heat tolerance, PS for a cheap clear look that will not survive a hard drop. A stadium running 20,000 cups a night usually lands on PP for beer and Tritan only where the drink has to be seen."},{"q":"Can unbreakable cups be printed and stacked?","a":"Yes, and stacking is the detail that catches people out: print placed where cups nest rubs off against the cup below. Keep the decoration in the upper band, or specify a stacking lug, and the print survives transport and service."}],"/blank-drinkware/":[{"q":"What decoration methods work on your blanks?","a":"Heat transfer, UV DTF wrap and direct print all work on the rigid plastic bodies; sublimation needs a coated surface, which is a separate specification and not something to assume. Tell us which method your shop runs and we confirm the compatible body before you buy a pallet."},{"q":"Can blanks be ordered in mixed colours?","a":"Yes, on an existing mould, with the per-colour minimum applying to each colour rather than to the order. A mixed pallet of five colours is five colour minimums. That is why resellers usually start with two or three colours and add as they learn what sells."},{"q":"Do blanks come with lids and straws?","a":"Only if the model is specified with them. A lid is a separate moulded part and is quoted as a component. If you resell blanks, decide early whether you are selling the cup alone or a ready-to-fill set, because the packing and the carton count change with it."},{"q":"Is there a minimum for a blank order?","a":"Ten pieces on a stock body and stock colour. Larger blank orders are quoted with a price ladder because the economics are almost entirely about quantity and carton efficiency. Ask for the ladder at 500, 1,000 and 5,000 so you can see where it flattens."}],"/16-oz-custom-cups/":[{"q":"Is 16 oz the same as a pint?","a":"A US pint is 16 US fluid ounces, which is 473 ml. A UK pint is 568 ml, so a 16 oz cup is not a UK pint and will be short by about 95 ml. If the cup is for a measured pour, specify millilitres and check the fill line rather than trusting the ounce label."},{"q":"How much headroom does a 16 oz cup need?","a":"Fill to roughly 80 to 85 percent for a beer with a head, which leaves about 70 to 95 ml of space. That is the reason a 16 oz cup is often sold as a 14 oz pour. Choose the size from the pour, not from the name on the cup."},{"q":"Which lid fits a 16 oz cup?","a":"The lid that was moulded for that specific rim. Rim diameters look identical between models and are not. When you order lids with cups, both go on the same specification so the fit is our responsibility; when you buy lids elsewhere, send a cup sample so we can match the rim."},{"q":"Can 16 oz cups be printed for a one-night event?","a":"Yes, that is one of the most common orders we run: a stock 16 oz body, one or two colours, a few hundred pieces. Ask for the lead time at that quantity early — printing is quick, but a colour match on a short run can add days."}],"/color-changing-cups/":[{"q":"How does the colour change work?","a":"A thermochromic ink layer sits over a base colour and turns transparent as the temperature drops, revealing what is underneath. No battery, no electronics. The effect is triggered by the cold drink, not by the air, so an empty cup on a table stays in its warm state."},{"q":"What is the minimum for colour-change cups?","a":"250 pieces. That is the colour-change tier, and it exists because the ink is mixed and laid down as a separate pass with its own set-up. Below 250 the ink cost per cup stops making sense, which is why this line does not start at ten like the standard print lines."},{"q":"How long does the colour-change layer last?","a":"It is a printed layer, so it ages like print: hand washing is fine, a commercial dishwasher will shorten it, and abrasive sponges will wear the surface. Treat it as a promotional cup with a realistic service life rather than as a permanent finish."},{"q":"Can the hidden colour be a full image?","a":"Yes, within the resolution the ink pass allows. Simple shapes, two-tone graphics and text hold well; photographs turn muddy because the reveal happens through a thin layer. Send the artwork as vector where possible and we will tell you what will read clearly at the moment of change."}],"/glow-in-the-dark-cups/":[{"q":"How does the glow work without a battery?","a":"Photoluminescent pigment in the material or the print absorbs light and releases it slowly in the dark. It charges from any light source and fades over minutes to hours depending on the pigment load. It is not the same as an LED cup, which is powered."},{"q":"How bright is it in a real venue?","a":"Dim, not bright. A glow cup reads clearly in a dark room after charging and disappears under venue lighting. If the goal is a visible effect across a crowd, an LED cup does that job; if the goal is a subtle glow on a table at the end of the night, glow pigment is the cheaper and simpler answer."},{"q":"Is the glowing material safe to drink from?","a":"Where the pigment is in the print, it sits on the outside of the body and never touches the drink. Where the pigment is moulded into the material, the body must be a food-contact grade compound. Ask which of the two you are buying — they are priced differently and they are not interchangeable."},{"q":"What is the minimum order for glow cups?","a":"Ten pieces if the body exists and only the print changes. A body moulded in glow compound is a new material run, so it follows the tooling threshold. Tell us whether you need the whole cup to glow or just the logo."}],"/custom-coasters/":[{"q":"Which coaster material suits a bar?","a":"Silicone for grip and no-slip on a wet bar top, rigid plastic for a printed surface that takes fine detail, and either for a giveaway. Silicone flexes and survives being dropped; rigid plastic holds a crisp logo but can skid on a wet surface unless it has a foot or a texture."},{"q":"What thickness should a coaster be?","a":"Three to four millimetres for a bar coaster that has to absorb a glass being put down hard, two millimetres for a printed promotional piece. Thinner than two millimetres and the coaster curls at the edge after a few washes. Ask for the thickness in millimetres, not as thin or standard."},{"q":"Can coasters be printed edge to edge?","a":"On a flat rigid coaster, yes, within a small border. On silicone the print sits in a recessed or textured area, so full bleed is limited. Both need the artwork at final size with the bleed marked; scaling a logo up on a coaster is the most common reason a first proof looks wrong."},{"q":"What is the smallest coaster order?","a":"Ten pieces on an existing coaster mould with a print change. Coasters are cheap to run and expensive to set up, so the price per piece falls fast: a few hundred pieces is usually where a promotional coaster starts to make sense commercially."}],"/zh/custom-ice-buckets/":[{"q":"桌面服务该选多大容量的冰桶？","a":"5 升左右的桶适合 2 到 4 瓶的桌边服务，冰能围住瓶身；10 升是多数酒吧一整班用的规格。定之前先量瓶身直径和桶口内径对不对得上——口窄肚大的桶，插香槟瓶并不好用。"},{"q":"桶身外面印了图案，里面还能直接放冰和饮品吗？","a":"可以。图案只做在外壁，内壁保持材料本色，不上油墨也不做涂层。凡是装冰、装饮品、接触食物的部件，我们都不在内侧印刷；如果设计上要求印内侧，我们会在书面上说明并建议改方案。"},{"q":"亚克力和 PP 怎么选？","a":"亚克力通透，接近玻璃的观感，适合摆台、能看清冰块；PP 不透明、更轻，走量的促销单成本更低。这一页两种都有。先按\"这桶会不会被摆出来看\"来选，再按型号确认容量和装箱。"},{"q":"冰桶能配盖子和内衬吗？","a":"部分型号有盖或内衬，部分没有——这是逐个型号的事，不是整页通用。你把看中的型号告诉我们，我们确认配件、盖子的配合和内衬的包装方式，然后再做样。"},{"q":"一张订单能同时混发光款和不发光款吗？","a":"可以。发光和不发光的桶可以放在同一张订单里，各自按自己的起订量算。装箱按型号分开，方便分别发往不同场地或包厢。"},{"q":"印 logo 的冰桶起订量是多少？","a":"现有模具的现货款，按型号起订可以做印刷或换色；新开模 10,000 件起。具体数字看你选哪一款，以报价单为准，不按这一页估算。"},{"q":"塑料冰桶上的 logo 用什么工艺？","a":"常用丝印和 UV 打印，图案颜色多的时候用热转印。能印的是桶身的平面区域；桶身有弧度或带棱线的，可印范围会比看上去小，正式生产前在打样稿上确认。"},{"q":"能按活动日期安排生产吗？","a":"告诉我们货必须到的那一天和送到哪里，生产和运输按这个日期倒排，外箱可以按场地分开贴标。加急能不能做逐单确认，不在页面上打包票。"}],"/zh/led-light-up-cups/":[{"q":"印了 logo 的发光杯，电源和灯效能统一吗？","a":"不按统一配置假设。先说你想要的灯效、开关方式和供电偏好，再逐个型号确认。logo 要在灯亮和灯不亮两种状态下都看一遍。"},{"q":"开关方式和供电该怎么提要求？","a":"把使用场景、想要的效果和供电偏好讲清楚，我们按型号确认具体配置和操作说明，再定样。"},{"q":"哪些发光杯款可以印 logo？","a":"啤酒杯、岩石杯、子弹杯、高脚杯和随行杯，只要杯身有平整的可印面，都能印。杯身有弧度或切面的，实际可印面积比看上去小，以打样稿确认为准。"},{"q":"灯怎么开？","a":"按型号不同：有按键的、有旋转底座的、也有拔插片的。定稿前先确认所选型号的开关方式，因为它决定 logo 能放在哪里。"},{"q":"发光杯用什么供电？","a":"供电按型号区分——纽扣电池、密封电池仓、可充电的都有。报价前问清所选型号的配置、电池能不能换，再排多天的活动。"},{"q":"发光杯的起订量是多少？","a":"现货款 按型号起订；换杯型或改灯光位置要重新开模，10,000 件起。具体数字以你选的型号和灯效为准，写在报价单上。"}]};function s(a){var e=document.createElement("details"),o=document.createElement("summary");o.textContent=a.q;var t=document.createElement("p");return t.textContent=a.a,e.appendChild(o),e.appendChild(t),e}function d(a){var e=document.createElement("section");e.className="bx-wrap bx-section bx-faq-restore";var o=document.createElement("h2");o.textContent=location.pathname.indexOf("/zh/")===0?"常见问题":"Common questions",e.appendChild(o);var t=document.createElement("div");return t.className="opt-faq",a.forEach(function(i){t.appendChild(s(i))}),e.appendChild(t),e}function u(a){if(!(!a||!a.length)&&!document.querySelector(".bx-faq-restore")){var e=document.querySelector(".bx-qa");if(e&&e.querySelector("details")){[].slice.call(e.querySelectorAll("details")).forEach(function(n){n.remove()}),a.forEach(function(n){e.appendChild(s(n))}),window.__ecApplySchema&&window.__ecApplySchema();return}var o=document.querySelector(".bx-model-selector"),t=d(a);if(o)o.insertAdjacentElement("afterend",t);else{var i=document.querySelectorAll("main .bx-section, .bx-site > .bx-section"),l=i[i.length-1];if(l)l.insertAdjacentElement("afterend",t);else{var h=document.querySelector("main")||document.querySelector(".bx-site");if(!h)return;h.appendChild(t)}}window.__ecApplySchema&&window.__ecApplySchema()}}function r(){u(c[location.pathname.replace(/\/?$/,"/")])}document.readyState==="complete"?setTimeout(r,900):window.addEventListener("load",function(){setTimeout(r,900)})})();
